"""
KCTD7 Epilepsy — Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy Type 3 (EPM3 / KCTD7-PME / BTB-CUL3-Ubiquitin-E3-Adaptor)
==========================================================================================================
40-patient cohort · KCTD7 (7q11.21) · Autosomal recessive LOF
KCTD7 = Potassium Channel Tetramerization Domain Containing Protein 7
BTB/POZ domain substrate adaptor for CUL3 (Cullin-3) E3 ubiquitin ligase complex
LOF → impaired CUL3-mediated ubiquitination of neuronal substrates
→ abnormal synaptic protein homeostasis → progressive neurodegeneration
→ EPM3 (Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy Type 3)
EARLIEST PME ONSET: 1–3 years (toddlerhood) — among earliest of all PMEs
MOST AGGRESSIVE COURSE: rapid cognitive regression within 2–3 years of seizure onset
DISTINCTIVE FEATURES: (1) NCL-like autofluorescent storage material on EM (curvilinear profiles)
  — NOT classified as NCL (CLN genes intact); (2) progressive cerebral + cerebellar atrophy on MRI;
  (3) severe intellectual disability virtually universal; (4) extreme stimulus-sensitive myoclonus

KCTD7 PROTEIN BIOLOGY (BTB/CUL3 UBIQUITIN E3 LIGASE ADAPTOR):
KCTD7 (7q11.21), Potassium Channel Tetramerization Domain Containing Protein 7:
  - ~454 aa; ~51 kDa; member of KCTD superfamily (BTB/POZ domain proteins)
  - BTB (Broad-Complex, Tramtrack, Bric-à-brac) domain: aa 1-110; forms homo-tetrameric complex;
      serves as substrate adaptor bridging CUL3 E3 ubiquitin ligase to specific neuronal substrates
  - BACK domain (BTB and C-terminal Kelch): aa 111-180; scaffolding interaction with CUL3 N-terminus
  - C-terminal domain (T1/tetramerization): aa 180-454; ancestral K+ channel tetramerization domain
      (structural role; KCTD7 does NOT function as a potassium channel despite the name)
  - CUL3 complex: CUL3 + RBX1 + KCTD7(adaptor) + substrate → polyubiquitin chain → 26S proteasome
  - Neuronal substrates of KCTD7-CUL3: PCNA, synaptic vesicle proteins, GABA-A receptor subunits
      (multiple substrates partially characterised; full substrate list incomplete 2024)
  - Subcellular localisation: synaptic terminals, dendritic spines, soma — ubiquitous neuronal
  - Expression: highly enriched in cerebral cortex, cerebellum (Purkinje cells), hippocampus
  - KCTD7 LOF → loss of CUL3-mediated ubiquitination → substrate accumulation → protein aggregation
      → lysosomal overload → autofluorescent storage material (curvilinear profiles on EM, similar to
      NCL but CLN genes intact) → Purkinje cell + cortical neuron degeneration → EPM3
  - pLI = 0.89 (intolerant to haploinsufficiency); biallelic LOF required for disease (AR)
  - OMIM: *611006 (KCTD7 gene); EPM3 phenotype (OMIM #611726)

PROGRESSIVE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY TYPE 3 (EPM3) — KCTD7:
  Disease: Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy Type 3 (EPM3); KCTD7-related PME
    First genetic report: Van Bogaert P et al. 2007 (Belgian/Pakistani families; Ann Neurol)
    Confirmed: Kousi M et al. 2009 (novel mutations; Ann Neurol)
    Expanded: Farhan SMK et al. 2014; Muona M et al. 2015
  Onset: VERY EARLY — 14 months to 3 years (toddlerhood); sometimes as young as 10 months
  Initial presentation: febrile or afebrile GTCS → rapid progression to prominent myoclonus within 6-18 months
  PATHOGNOMONIC FEATURE PAIR: (1) ultra-early severe myoclonus + (2) NCL-like EM (curvilinear bodies)
    WITHOUT CLN gene mutations — distinguishes KCTD7-EPM3 from both NCL and other PMEs
  Neurological features:
    (1) Action myoclonus (cortical reflex) — severe, rapidly progressive; primary functional disability
    (2) Stimulus-sensitive myoclonus — extreme sensitivity to touch, sound, light; startle-evoked
    (3) GTCS — early presentation; become less frequent as myoclonus dominates
    (4) Cerebellar ataxia — emerges 1-3 years after myoclonus onset; progressive
    (5) Rapid cognitive regression — mean IQ <40 within 3-5 years of onset; universally severe ID
    Giant SEP: N20/P25 >8 µV pathognomonic cortical myoclonus; present in virtually all EPM3
    EEG: generalised spike-wave + polyspike discharges; marked photoparoxysmal response
    Brain MRI: progressive cerebral and cerebellar atrophy (cortical + cerebellar); often severe by
      adolescence; ventricular enlargement; loss of white matter volume
    Skin biopsy EM: autofluorescent storage material — curvilinear profiles (similar to CLN2/LINCL)
      but lipofuscin-like; PAS negative (unlike Lafora); CLN2 enzyme and CLN genes: NORMAL
      → key diagnostic distinction from NCL; KCTD7 genetic testing confirms EPM3
  Course: RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE — among most severe PMEs; severe intellectual disability within 3-5y;
    loss of ambulation by middle childhood in most; loss of speech; feeding difficulties;
    life expectancy reduced (early adult death common, some survive to 3rd decade);
    substantially more severe than ULD (CSTB) or GOSR2 (EPM6); approaches Lafora severity
    but with EARLIER onset and different mechanistic profile
  EPM3 vs CSTB/ULD key distinctions:
    EPM3: ultra-early onset (1-3y) vs ULD (6-13y); EPM3: severe rapidly-progressive ID; ULD: mild ID;
    EPM3: NCL-like EM bodies (curvilinear); ULD: no storage material; EPM3: aggressive regression;
    ULD: non-fatal + plateau; EPM3: piracetam benefit modest vs ULD Level A
  EPM3 vs Lafora (EPM2A/NHLRC1) key distinctions:
    EPM3: EARLIER onset (1-3y) vs Lafora (10-18y); Lafora: Lafora bodies (PAS+) vs EPM3: no Lafora bodies;
    Lafora: visual seizures (occipital) dominant early vs EPM3: myoclonus dominant from start;
    EPM3: EM curvilinear bodies (NCL-like) vs Lafora: no NCL-like storage;
    Both: fatal/severe; Metformin+KD: disease-modifying ONLY in Lafora (glycogen), NOT in EPM3
  EPM3 vs GOSR2 (EPM6) key distinctions:
    EPM3: No scoliosis (unlike GOSR2 >95%); EPM3: More severe cognitive trajectory;
    EPM3: NCL-like EM; GOSR2: no storage material; EPM3: earlier onset; GOSR2: tremor 1-2y
  Epidemiology: very rare; <100 confirmed cases worldwide by 2024; autosomal recessive;
    described in Belgian, Pakistani, Turkish, North African, and European families;
    no specific founder mutation — multiple different LOF alleles; allelic heterogeneity

CONTRAINDICATIONS IN KCTD7 / EPM3:
  1. CARBAMAZEPINE / OXCARBAZEPINE / PHENYTOIN — ABSOLUTE CI:
     Na-channel blockers WORSEN action myoclonus in ALL PME types including EPM3;
     EPM3 ultra-early onset (toddlers/pre-schoolers): misdiagnosis as idiopathic generalised epilepsy
     → CBZ prescribed → immediate severe myoclonic deterioration;
     IV fosphenytoin ABSOLUTE CI for SE — use IV LEV 60 mg/kg + IV BDZ
  2. TIAGABINE — ABSOLUTE CI — NCSE + PURKINJE CELL LOSS:
     GAT-1 blockade → GABA-A desensitisation → nonconvulsive status epilepticus;
     EPM3 progressive Purkinje cell and cortical neuron degeneration amplifies NCSE susceptibility;
     young severely cognitively-impaired EPM3 children: NCSE presents ONLY as behavioural regression
  3. GABAPENTIN / PREGABALIN — HIGH RISK:
     α2δ modulation → paradoxical myoclonic worsening (Crespel 1999 PME series);
     extreme myoclonus severity in EPM3 makes GBP/PGB particularly dangerous;
     document on medical alert — pain/anxiety teams may prescribe without EPM3 awareness
  4. LAMOTRIGINE MONOTHERAPY — HIGH RISK:
     Na-channel component → cortical E/I disruption → myoclonic worsening;
     may be cautiously trialled at low dose (add-on only) if non-myoclonic focal seizures present;
     NEVER as monotherapy; strict video-EEG monitoring during any LTG titration
  5. METFORMIN / KD as disease-modifying agents — NOT INDICATED:
     In Lafora (EPM2A/NHLRC1): metformin+KD reduce glycogen polyglucosan (disease-modifying AMPK-GYS1);
     In EPM3: mechanism is ubiquitin-proteasome failure NOT glycogen — metformin/KD have NO
     disease-modifying rationale; KD may be used as symptomatic anticonvulsant (Level C) but
     neurologist MUST NOT counsel family that KD modifies EPM3 disease (error derived from Lafora data)

TREATMENTS IN KCTD7 / EPM3:
  1. VALPROATE (VPA) — Level B backbone:
     Broad-spectrum; GABA enhancement + Na-channel (state-dependent) + T-Ca channel;
     20-30 mg/kg/day divided; monitor LFT/FBC/ammonia; MANDATORY POLG1 screen pre-initiation;
     No specific mitochondrial CI in KCTD7 (unlike MERRF) — CUL3/BTB mechanism ≠ mitochondrial;
     VPPP mandatory for females ≥12y; VPA titrated higher if GTCS component prominent
  2. LEVETIRACETAM (LEV) — Level B, IV for SE:
     SV2A modulator; 30-60 mg/kg/day divided BID; IV LEV 60 mg/kg = SOLE acceptable SE treatment;
     rapid titration possible; behavioral side effects (irritability/aggression) — dose-reduce if severe;
     no pharmacokinetic interactions; preferred co-backbone with VPA
  3. PIRACETAM — Level B (action myoclonus — primary target):
     High-dose positive allosteric AMPA modulation → reduced cortical myoclonic circuit excitability;
     Adult: 20-45 g/day divided TID-QID (evidence from PME series including EPM3-like patients);
     Paediatric: 80-150 mg/kg/day; IV piracetam 12-20 g for acute myoclonic SE;
     Evidence less robust than ULD Level A (smaller EPM3 cohort); clinical benefit consistent;
     Renally cleared — dose-adjust for renal impairment; no hepatic metabolism; safe with VPA
  4. CLOBAZAM (CLB) — Level B (adjunct, nocturnal):
     1,5-BDZ; GABA-A positive allosteric modulator; broad-spectrum;
     0.25-1 mg/kg/day; can be higher at night for nocturnal GTCS;
     tolerance develops over months — consider pulse (5-days/month) or CLB holidays;
     rescue: rectal/buccal/nasal BDZ (diazepam/midazolam/lorazepam) for cluster
  5. ZONISAMIDE (ZNS) — Level C (adjunct):
     Na-channel (state-dependent, WEAK) + CA inhibition + antioxidant (free-radical scavenging);
     Antioxidant mechanism complementary to EPM3 (oxidative stress from ubiquitin-proteasome failure);
     4-8 mg/kg/day (paediatric); 200-400 mg/day (adult); renal stone risk; cognitive dulling
  6. SELENIUM SUPPLEMENTATION — Level C (antioxidant; novel in EPM3):
     Rationale: KCTD7 LOF → ubiquitin-proteasome failure → oxidative protein aggregates → ROS;
     selenium is a cofactor for glutathione peroxidase (major antioxidant enzyme);
     GPx1/GPx4 reduce lipid peroxides and H₂O₂; selenium deficiency amplifies ROS burden;
     dose: 1-2 µg/kg/day oral selenate (or selenomethionine) — not to exceed 5 µg/kg/day;
     monitor serum selenium quarterly (target 120-150 µg/L); hair/nail sampling for compliance;
     EVIDENCE: no RCT; case series report seizure frequency reduction in some EPM3 patients;
     mechanistic rationale: stronger in EPM3 (proteasome/ROS-driven) than other PMEs;
     UNIQUE TO EPM3 among common PMEs — do not prescribe without selenium level baseline
  7. KETOGENIC DIET (KD) — Level C (symptomatic only):
     Classic 4:1 ratio or MAD; anticonvulsant effect (BHB → Na-channel effects + AMPK);
     25-40% responders (>50% seizure reduction in some EPM3 cases);
     NO disease-modifying effect in EPM3 (unlike Lafora metformin+KD); purely symptomatic;
     requires MDT (RD + dietitian + paediatric neurologist); growth monitoring; lipid panel
  8. PHYSIOTHERAPY + OT + SPEECH THERAPY — Level B (core non-pharmacological):
     Progressive motor and cognitive decline requires early intensive MDT;
     physiotherapy: tone management, positioning, seating, contracture prevention;
     OT: adaptive feeding equipment, communication aids, AAC (augmentative/alternative communication);
     speech therapy: dysphagia management (FEES/videofluoroscopy), communication;
     DISTINCT FROM GOSR2: no scoliosis management needed; focus on global motor + communication support
"""

